# CHAPTER 2 — Review of Related Literature and Studies

## Introduction

This chapter presents the review of related literature and studies that provide the foundation for the development of the Smart Hybrid Library Management and Automation System (SHLMAS). It discusses existing concepts, technologies, and previous research related to library management systems, library automation, RFID technology, and low-cost technology solutions in educational environments.

The reviewed literature establishes the importance of improving library circulation processes through digital solutions. It also identifies existing limitations of current library automation approaches, particularly regarding cost, infrastructure requirements, and suitability for public schools with limited resources.

Furthermore, this chapter presents the theoretical framework, conceptual framework, and research gap that explain the relationship between existing knowledge and the proposed SHLMAS. The study is positioned as a low-cost RFID-assisted library automation approach designed to reduce manual recording procedures and improve circulation management in public school libraries.

