# 1. Related Literature

## Library Management Systems

A Library Management System (LMS) is an integrated information system designed to support and automate essential library operations, including cataloging, circulation management, user management, and reporting. These systems allow libraries to organize resources efficiently, maintain accurate records, and provide faster access to information resources (Asemi et al., 2010).

One of the primary functions of a Library Management System is circulation management, which handles activities such as borrowing, returning, renewing, and monitoring library materials. Traditional circulation processes often rely on handwritten logbooks, manual registers, or paper-based records. Although these methods are simple to implement, they become increasingly inefficient as the number of library users and transactions increases.

Digital circulation systems improve library operations by storing transaction records electronically. Instead of manually searching through physical records, library personnel can retrieve borrower information, transaction history, and book availability through a computerized system. Digital record management also reduces the risk of missing entries, inaccurate records, and difficulties in monitoring borrowed materials (Bansode & Periera, 2008).

However, many public schools continue to rely on manual circulation procedures due to limited financial resources, lack of technical infrastructure, and insufficient access to specialized library management technologies. These limitations highlight the need for affordable and practical automation solutions suitable for resource-constrained educational environments.

