## Library Automation

Library automation refers to the application of computer-based technologies to perform library activities that were traditionally completed manually. It involves the use of software and hardware systems to improve processes such as cataloging, circulation, inventory management, and information retrieval (Singh, 2003).

The implementation of automated systems provides several advantages for library operations. First, automation improves processing efficiency by reducing the time required to complete repetitive tasks such as recording borrower information and updating book records. Second, it improves data accuracy by minimizing human errors commonly associated with manual encoding. Third, automation allows library personnel to focus more on user assistance and educational support rather than spending significant time on administrative record keeping (Singh, 2003).

Modern libraries commonly utilize technologies such as Integrated Library Systems (ILS), Online Public Access Catalogs (OPAC), barcode systems, and RFID-based identification systems. These technologies allow faster access to information and improve the overall management of library resources.

Despite these benefits, the adoption of advanced library automation systems remains challenging in many public educational institutions. Unlike universities and private organizations with larger technology budgets, public schools often experience limitations in funding, hardware availability, internet connectivity, and technical support. As a result, there is a need for automation solutions that prioritize affordability, simplicity, and practical implementation.
